Chapter: GopFace
  cites Foreword chapter MathLib
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopComposite

 An analogue clock face: the dial, its ticks, and three hands.

 Stage 3 of `docs/Designs/Active/Apps/ClockAndZone.md`.

 It draws through `comp-blend-px`, which takes a clip and bounds every write,
 rather than through `gop-put` directly. `gop-put` has no bottom edge, and a
 face is the first thing on this desk drawn from an angle rather than from a
 rectangle: the arithmetic that places a hand tip cannot be read off the
 caller's box, so a bound that is a rectangle the caller passed is the only
 one worth having.

Section: The circle

 SIXTY POSITIONS AND NOT AN ANGLE, because a clock has exactly sixty of them
 and every one is a whole number of degrees. A second is six degrees, so the
 quarter turn is fifteen steps and a table of sixteen sines covers the whole
 dial by symmetry with no interpolation and no error to reason about.

 The values are sine times 1000 at 0, 6, 12 ... 90 degrees. They are a table
 rather than a series because a series here would be a slower way to get the
 same sixteen numbers, and the numbers are what a reader wants to check.

  face-sin-q : Integer -> Integer
  face-sin-q (i) =
    if i <= 0 then 0
    else if i == 1 then 105 else if i == 2 then 208 else if i == 3 then 309
    else if i == 4 then 407 else if i == 5 then 500 else if i == 6 then 588
    else if i == 7 then 669 else if i == 8 then 743 else if i == 9 then 809
    else if i == 10 then 866 else if i == 11 then 914 else if i == 12 then 951
    else if i == 13 then 978 else if i == 14 then 995 else 1000

 `t` counts clockwise from twelve o'clock, which is what a clock does and is
 not what mathematics does. Twelve is straight up, so the vertical component
 is NEGATIVE at t = 0: the screen's y grows downward and the hand points up.

  face-sin : Integer -> Integer
  face-sin (t) =
    let n = int-mod (int-mod t 60 + 60) 60
    in if n <= 15 then face-sin-q n
    else if n <= 30 then face-sin-q (30 - n)
    else if n <= 45 then 0 - face-sin-q (n - 30)
    else 0 - face-sin-q (60 - n)

  face-cos : Integer -> Integer
  face-cos (t) = face-sin (t + 15)

  face-dx : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-dx (t) (r) = face-sin t * r / 1000

  face-dy : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-dy (t) (r) = 0 - (face-cos t * r / 1000)

Section: Marks

 A DISC RATHER THAN A PIXEL, so a hand has width and a tick has weight.

 THE RIM IS THE ONLY PART THAT COSTS A ROOT. A circle drawn by the squared
 test alone is a staircase, and a staircase on a dial is the same defect the
 rounded corners had: it reads as a badly drawn shape rather than a smooth
 one. But taking a root per pixel to antialias a disc would take one for every
 pixel of the dial, and the dial is the largest thing on the pane.

 So the interior and the exterior are decided by the squared test, which is
 exact and cheap, and only the one pixel band between `(rr-1)^2` and `(rr+1)^2`
 is measured. That band is the circumference, which grows with the radius
 while the area grows with its square: the cost of the smoothing is a line and
 not a disc.

  face-cov : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-cov (d2) (rr) =
    let v = rr * 256 + 128 - math-isqrt (d2 * 65536)
    in if v <= 0 then 0 else if v > 255 then 255 else v

  face-disc-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-disc-row (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (rr) (y) (x) (col) =
    if x > cx + rr + 1 then 0
    else
      let dx = x - cx
      in let dy = y - cy
      in let d2 = dx * dx + dy * dy
      in let a = if d2 <= (rr - 1) * (rr - 1) then 255
            else if d2 > (rr + 1) * (rr + 1) then 0
            else face-cov d2 rr
      in let d = if a <= 0 then 0 else comp-blend-px base stride rows clip x y col a
      in face-disc-row base stride rows clip cx cy rr y (x + 1) col

  face-disc-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-disc-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (rr) (y) (col) =
    if y > cy + rr + 1 then 0
    else
      let one = face-disc-row base stride rows clip cx cy rr y (cx - rr - 1) col
      in face-disc-at base stride rows clip cx cy rr (y + 1) col

  face-disc : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-disc (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (rr) (col) =
    if rr <= 0 then comp-blend-px base stride rows clip cx cy col 255
    else face-disc-at base stride rows clip cx cy rr (cy - rr - 1) col

 A HAND IS A WALK AND NOT A LINE EQUATION. It steps out from the centre one
 device pixel at a time along the same sixty-position table the ticks use, so
 a hand lands exactly on the tick it points at. A Bresenham line would land
 near it, and near is what makes an analogue clock look wrong.

 The step is the radius in pixels, so the walk is as long as the hand and no
 longer, and the cost is a disc per pixel of hand rather than per pixel of
 dial.

  face-hand-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-hand-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (t) (i) (n) (wid) (col) =
    if i > n then 0
    else
      let x = cx + face-dx t i
      in let y = cy + face-dy t i
      in let one = face-disc base stride rows clip x y wid col
      in face-hand-at base stride rows clip cx cy t (i + 1) n wid col

  face-hand : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-hand (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (t) (from) (to) (wid) (col) =
    face-hand-at base stride rows clip cx cy t from to wid col

Section: The dial

 EVERY FIFTH TICK IS LONGER, which is the only thing that makes a dial
 readable at a glance: without it a viewer counts marks instead of seeing
 hours. The long ticks are also drawn thicker, because at this size length
 alone does not separate them.

  face-tick-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-tick-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (r) (t) (col) (hour-col) =
    if t >= 60 then 0
    else
      let big = int-mod t 5 == 0
      in let inner = if big then r * 82 / 100 else r * 90 / 100
      in let wid = if big then 1 else 0
      in let c = if big then hour-col else col
      in let one = face-hand base stride rows clip cx cy t inner r wid c
      in face-tick-at base stride rows clip cx cy r (t + 1) col hour-col

 The hour hand advances WITH the minutes, which is the difference between a
 clock and a picture of one: at half past ten the hour hand sits between ten
 and eleven, and a hand that jumped on the hour reads as broken to anyone who
 has looked at a real one.

 Sixty positions cover twelve hours at five per hour, so the hour position is
 `h mod 12` times five plus the minutes divided by twelve.

  face-hour-t : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-hour-t (h) (mi) = int-mod h 12 * 5 + mi / 12

 Drawn back to front: dial, ticks, then the hands in the order a viewer
 expects to find them on top of one another, and the hub last so that three
 hands meeting at a point look like one joint rather than three overlaps.

 The hands are given radii as PERCENTAGES of the dial, so the face is one
 shape at any size and a caller that halves the radius gets the same clock.

  face-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  face-draw (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (cx) (cy) (r) (h) (mi) (s) (rim) (tick) (hand) (accent) =
    let d0 = face-disc base stride rows clip cx cy r rim
    in let d1 = face-tick-at base stride rows clip cx cy (r * 94 / 100) 0 tick hand
    in let d2 = face-hand base stride rows clip cx cy (face-hour-t h mi) 0 (r * 50 / 100) 2 hand
    in let d3 = face-hand base stride rows clip cx cy (int-mod mi 60) 0 (r * 74 / 100) 1 hand
    in let d4 = face-hand base stride rows clip cx cy (int-mod s 60) 0 (r * 84 / 100) 0 accent
    in let d5 = face-disc base stride rows clip cx cy 3 hand
    in face-disc base stride rows clip cx cy 1 accent

